Hi all,

It's my first time posting here, so you won't know me unless you were
on #nethack the few times I did say something.  Nevertheless, I read
the group daily or so.  In the name of the other lurkers: thanks for
being there :-)

That particular game spread over a whole month in real world time; I
will probably mix up a fair count of events.  My notes keep a lot of
location-related information, and just about nothing pertaining to
time.  I extracted a little information out of my IRC logs (e.g. when
was that time I was polymorphed, reported in the endgame).  I'll edit
my personal record if I manage to get a copy of the ttyrecs.




I, a newly trained Plunderess, had been one more heralded from birth
time as the instrument of Set, blah blah blah.

The game started well: 
  * battle-axe -- so much less of an experience slot waste then the
                  two-handed sword!
  * a nice pet -- I honestly don't remember, let's assume for the
                  story's stake that it was the usual little dog named
                  Idefix
  * (cross-)aligned altar on Dlvl 1

The Gnomish Mines forked off Dlvl 2, but I usually try Sokoban first.
Sokoban's entrance was in Dlvl 6.  The reward was a bag of holding,
which is what I prize most at that time when I don't already have
one.  I converted an altar on Dlvl 3 on the way to the mines.

I don't remember much about them.  This most probably means the temple
was cross-aligned, and that mine's end was the mimic of the mines.  Go
down, fetch and #name stones, get out with full dwarvish armor and a
partially identified inventory.

A bones pile awaited me on Dlvl 11 (thanks, robbi!), with various
stuff, most notably an uncursed bag of holding and a wand of
cancellation.

I received a faint telepathic message from Pelias as I set foot on
Dlvl 12.  I sought the ...ic transporter on the whole level, but
stepped on a polymorph trap one step before it.  I became a master
lich... which is kind of all right compared to what I've already been
changed to in former games.  HD 17 (I was XL13 as a human), no armor
broken, great AC and that cool frost attack.

Across the river, Pelias was waiting for me.  My master lich
appearance didn't impress him in the least, all he told me was "only a
raider could take on Thoth Amon and live."  Hey, old man, I'm HD17!
That's way better than a weak XL14 raider!

[15 minutes of source diving later, ] I quaffed a !oGL.  This was
enough to pass Pelias.  One level lower, my master lich shape expired,
and I didn't feel as confident about this whole quest business anymore
(the AC difference may explain a lot).  I cowarded out and went on
with the dungeon.

An aligned altar was expecting me on Dlvl 13, so I established my
outpost there.  robbi's bag of holding replaced a chest (none on that
level) and I sacrificed a few monsters passing by.  Set granted me
Cleaver (Cleaver is quite cool when you started the game with a battle
axe :-), the castle tune, and some useless intrinsic I don't remember.
Let's say Stealth, which barbarians eventually get anyway).

Downwards, the largest leprechaun hall I ever saw on Dlvl 15, the
rogue level on Dlvl16 (no big room), and another bones level some
place lower then.  Thanks to you, anonymous petrified adventurer,
whose statue I smashed before checking your name: you got me Mjollnir
(ouch! blasted), Stormbringer and Giantslayer.  Back to the stash, I
BUC test (Mjollnir was cursed), and switch from Cleaver to
Stormbringer to build up broadsword skill.  

I was about to sort out my inventory before going lower still, but I
decided to make one of the biggest mistakes of the game: I
(temporarily, right?) stashed my wand of cancellation, so the
inventory dump would be easier to read.  Boom!  Huh?  What happened?
Oh yeah, that's one of the issues to deal with when the game is so
spread out over time: really be careful about the things you don't do
completely as usual.  Oh, well... life goes on.  I hauled a real chest
from the level below and filled it with the bones level's artifacts,
which were luckily waiting on the altar when my stash exploded.

Throne room on Dlvl 21.  A silver dragon there generously dies
dropping scales!  Great!  Two ?oEA later, my AC looks like I can get
back to the quest!

Fate decided otherwise.  I was about to magic whistle Idefix across
the river, but the stupid dog started bugging the peaceful ogres on
the other bank.  "You have a sad feeling for a moment, then it
passes."  Oh well...  No way to revive him, and I'm not going down the
barbarian quest without a reliable way to keep trolls from
regenerating.  So I'm back in the dungeon, in search of that reliable
way.

I started looking for Ludios at that time.  It's just another thing to
do when you're just waiting for the generation of a tamable carnivore
monster.  I didn't find it, but I did collect a reasonable amount of
$.  Combined with the leprechaun hall's loot, it piled up in my bag,
so I set for minetown temple to invest it into something with a better
usefulness/weight ration.  

I had a Stormbringer accident on the watch captain there, but it was a
lucky pick: silver saber.  #twoweapon to build that one up too.  I
went to Izchak to try and replace the candles I had lost in the stash
accident, but he only had 5 of them.  2 wax and 3 tallow, IIRC, and I
don't know if you're allowed to mix them up in the end.  I bought them
anyway.

After some more wandering around, I finally found Medusa's on Dlvl 23
(no way of crossing), and a tinning kit some place above.  A tinning
kit is much heavier than a self-powered fast carnivore pet, but it's
an acceptable alternative to finish off troll corpses.

At last!  I went down the quest, tried to trick Thoth Amon by
teleporting him before he grabs the quest artifact (don't, it doesn't
work :-), and whacked him to death near the upstair.  Bell of Opening,
Heart of Ahriman; Good enough to proceed downwards.

Levitated to the island (thanks, Ahriman!), beat Medusa to death -- I
forgot to take a mirror from the stash -- and tinned her.  There!  Two
levels of mazes, then the castle Dlvl 26.  Oops, I forgot the musical
instrument too.  I really had a lot of fun with the drawbridge in my
previous game, so I went back up to get one.

The level above Medusa's: "You hear someone counting."  Ah-ha!
Pickaxe out, I locate the vault, and there it is!  "You activated a
magic portal."  Ludios with conflict and a tinning kit isn't that
hard, not much to say about it.

I'm back at the castle gate... and I still don't have a musical
instrument!  Oh well, this time at least I'll go around it (Ahriman
levitation) and loot the storage rooms.  A few interesting items, so I
run back to my stash for BUC/identification, then down again.  With a
bugle :-)

Play the passtune... nothing happens.  Oh, right, I'm too far away.
Get closer, play the passtune... there we go.  A soldier gets in
range, another one zaps striking from behind, and the drawbridge is
history.  I levitate in and slaughter all I can, conflict helping.
When only peacefuls are left, I loot the towers too and bring all I
can to base camp for a first wishing session.

A cloak of magic resistance!  At last!  A little enchantment, and it
looks like I can proceed to the valley.  I wiped out and looted all
three graveyards.  At the temple I tried some protection, and
purchased clairvoyance.  And here I am in the Gehennom mazes...

Two levels of mazes, then Juiblex.  He didn't swallow me, which
actually makes him harder to beat than when he does.  Under Juiblex,
Asmodeus got stoned by a Set-sent cockatrice's corpse.  Same fate for
Baalzebub in the following level.  Baalzebub is a nice demon, he comes
to greet you so you don't have to run after him.

Three more maze levels, dig through Orcus town floor, come back up,
kill him fast, collect the wand of death.  Two more maze levels... and
a master lich.  Back to upstairs quick, burn Elbereth...  Hey!  Where
are my wands?  Oops... forgot them at base camp.  Well, well, well,
fighting liches without Elbereth could turn out nasty.  Up to Dlvl13,
back down to Dlvl38 with all the wands I thought I'd need.

Vlad's tower was on the same level -- not much more to say.  The
wizard's tower spanned the following three levels, 39 to 41.  The
small towers were at 43 (guarded by an arch-lich, but it's less scary
with the wands) and 45.  But the first one was the right one: I
cleared out the landing room, and didn't bother to enter the other
one.

Vibrating square on level 46.  I marked up the spot the barbarian way,
destroying the surrounding walls.  Wizard of Yendor, here I come!

I whacked my way through the tower, conflicted the zoo. Then I paused
in the ladder's room and locked it tight.  Full main inventory review,
enchant all that's left, get /oDeath out.

I climbed the ladder upstairs. After I had killed every living being
in the rooms around the moat, he still wasn't after me, so I water
walked to blank and dilute my junk scrolls and potions.  While I was
there, I froze a passageway (slipped and fell a few times), stepped
back and put my speed boots back on.

I zapped digging; I zapped death and missed.  Missed the wizard, that
is, I did hit whatever was behind him.  I entered the small room to
see if Stormbringer would be any more effective.  I don't know if the
answer is that easy...  I finished him with a second /oDeath zap.
Picked up a papyrus spellbook, and levelported back to the VS.

Apply candles to vandelabrum.  Hmmm... looks like I forgot the candles
at base camp.  Oh, well... I didn't have enough anyway, so it's not
really a wasted wish.

I performed the invocation and dropped the items.  Stupid me, two of
them fell downstairs.  I walked down with the intent of picking them
up, and an arch-lich teleports next to me.  Oh well, who cares if I
litter the sanctum level's floor with invocation items? Back up I
run.

I grab my second cursed scroll of teleport from the bag... and pause
for a second.  I'm about to levelport down in the sanctum, an
arch-lich on the level, free to heal on the upstairs as often as he
pleases.  That's no good, I've got to finish him off at a time I
actually control the upstairs.  The scroll of teleport drops back in
the bag.  /oFire ready?  Okay, let's go.

I walked down again, and burned E on the upstair. Uh-oh, the arch-lich
isn't alone anymore, there's a minotaur and a disenchanter.  The
situation was so similar to that old Good/Bad/Ugly puzzle that I
paused for a second.

[ You know, the one where the Good succeeds one shot out of three, the
  Ugly one out of two, the Bad has 100% success; they shoot in the
  order I gave until only one remains; who should each of them aim at
  to maximize the odds of his own survival? ]

...but I was probably too excited anyway to make the best of it.  I
had conflict on, I decided to work on the most annoying one first,
leaving the others to whatever they wanted to do.  Select bare-handed
combat; hit the sucker.  "Your gauntlets of power seem less
effective".  Uh-oh, looks like I overlooked some detail.  "The
minotaur kills the disenchanter."  Oh, that's better.  Dual-wield
again, and finish off the arch-lich.

Now I'm back to business.  Collect the invocation items, climb back
up, and levelport down.  I land northwest of the sanctum, and there's
no one in sight, which was quite a surprise to me.  This gives me time
to remove the =oTC and put =oFA on instead.  I open my bag, reaching
in for my /oSecretDoorDetection... woops, looks like I forgot one more
item at base camp.

What else, then?  Hmm, I've got two charges left in this bell I wanted
to drop a few turns ago.  Let's make the best out of them.  I open my
bag again, take out two wands of light, and proceed to light the whole
area surrounding the sanctum.  Whacking a giant ant or two along the
way.  The entry was straight opposite from the point I landed at.

(come to think of it, I could probably have made much better use of
the candelabrum at that time)

Moloch's high priest was easy.  I tinned him for a souvenir, picked up
what I came for in the first place, locked the door, dropped the
invocation items, and quaffed that c!oGL I had been saving.

And up I go...  Run here, teleport there...  "The heat and smoke are
gone."  Oops, wrong path.  Run some more, teleport, dash through the
stoned demon princes' lairs.  Whoa, more gremlins!  Levitation on,
dodge through the swarm, up, up, up.

In the Valley of the Dead, I take the time to lock the doors to the
mazes.  I really wouldn't want an unwary walker to end up there by
accident.  I attempted to buy an additional point of AC in the temple,
but the priest just thought I was being generous.

Break a maze wall; fly in a straight line through the castle, break
another maze wall...  "Nothing happens."  Oh well, drop wand, get next
one out of... hmmm, no next one.  Fly around the wall, then.  Fly up,
way up to my stash.  (one encounter with the purple @)

Time the last inventory preparation session.  My /oWishing was down to
1:0 -- not that good for an emergency -- so I wrested out a last
blessed magic marker from it.  My /oDeath was down to 0:1, and I
hadn't encountered another one, so I wrote down two ?oCharging.  The
remaining magic ink went into enchant armor/weapon and gold detection.
Got the collected artifacts out of the chest, along with the gems.
Got out all the /oTeleport I'd found, the few useful potions
(confusion and paralysis), and alchemized the remainder to water and
blessed full healing.  No /oDigging left...  well, I suppose this
means I'll just have to go without.

I had some twenty ?oTeleport left, so I just used them on the way up.
My only pause was in a cabinet-sized corridor, to finish up my armor's
enchantment.  I'd never feel safe doing it elsewhere with the wizard
out to get me...  The wizard popped out one more time, at the middle
of Dlvl 1.

Earth.  Here's that wizard guy again.  Zap!  "The death ray misses
the wizard of Yendor!"  "The death ray bounces!"  "The wizard of
Yendor is killed!"

I count my ?oGoldDetection... and realize I'm short by one.  Oh,
well...  The magic mortal on this plane is as far as I suppose it can
get, but I don't feel like learning a spellbook /now/.  The guaranteed
/oDigging was almost enough, I just had a few chunks of earth to dig
out with the pickaxe.  Dropped useless digging-related, invoked the
Heart /beforehand/ this time... and jumped to the next plane.

Air.  Quite easy.  I only got engulfed by an air elemental once, just
long enough to realize I had forgotten my /oSleep/Slow at my stash.
(Oh well) Hit and strike, kill it.  A steam vortex was nice enough to
show me the way out, thus sparing the scroll I was missing.

Fire.  Very easy.  Just exactly _nothing_ interesting happened.

Water.  Kind of easy too.  I just had to slow myself down in the end
to be prepared for the last plane before getting there, and avoid my
previous game's fatal thus last mistakes.  Remove =oConflict, quaff
!oSpeed, put jumping boots on.

Astral.  I get spawned to the right side, so let's just go there.  I
don't remember even checking the assigned rider.

['P' ernyyl sryg yvxr purngvat ynfg gvzr, cyhf vg qvqa'g cnl, fb vg'f
ab-ab guvf bapr]

Jump, jump, teleport away, jump.  Oh, a purple & named Famine.  Check
nutrition level, and... what the heck... zap him to /death. Whack an
ant or two, jump, run, jump.  "There is an altar to Crom (neutral)
here."  [V'z punbgvp, fb V fhccbfr V pna'g rira ubcr sbe fbzr [bBN
pur... re, gvzr-fnivat urer.]

Jump, teleport away, jump, "Hi, Famine!", jump, zap, jump.  Middle
path was guarded by Death, and completely stuck by a long worm's tail.
I cut some chunks off to open the way, and actually didn't have to
take care of our &-friend, who was stuck behind w~~~.  Jump, teleport
away, jump, jump.  Conflict fight messages show me messages about some
"high-priest of Set".  Encouraging.  I was in the sanctuary, I took
=oConflict off.  "Pardon me, high priest of Set"  "There is an altar
to Set (chaotic) here."

Looks good.  What's this " sign next to me?  "Amulet of Yendor", eh?
Mine's still #named appropriately, I'm not falling in for this.  Two
magic lamps left, I tried to get some score out of them (believe it or
not, I hadn't ever rubbed a blessed magic lamp before).  Two puffs of
smoke later, all I could get was a "You disturbed me, fool!" and a
lousy *single* dilithium crystal. Ha!

"An invisible choir sings, and you are bathed in radiance..."
"The voice of Set booms: - Congratulations, mortal!"
"- In return for thy service, I grant thee the gift of Immortality!"
"You ascend to the status of Demigoddess..."



(I can't believe I wrote so much)

Whew!  A much better game than the previous one (d'uh!), but still
waaaaaay too many "oh, well" and roundtrips to the stash

Conflict sure rocks... maybe too much so?
Or is it just a too civilized way of doing?


